1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,742
Meet Eufisky, a voice guardian for older adults, built around Assembly AI.

2
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:13,398
The idea is simple: when an unfamiliar caller pressures someone, offer understandable help during the conversation.

3
00:00:13,398 --> 00:00:16,269
This submission demonstrates our browser prototype.

4
00:00:16,269 --> 00:00:23,925
The narrated recording includes a scripted Replay, and does not claim to measure live provider performance.

5
00:00:24,925 --> 00:00:25,866
Consider Margaret.

6
00:00:25,866 --> 00:00:31,515
A stranger calls claiming that her Medicare benefits will be suspended today.

7
00:00:31,515 --> 00:00:38,105
The danger develops after she answers, through urgency, threats, and requests for personal information.

8
00:00:38,105 --> 00:00:47,519
Eufisky explores a practical response: recognize those signals, explain the concern, and create a path to a trusted family member.

9
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:54,579
Our objective is support at the moment of pressure, while preserving the older adult's choice.

10
00:00:55,579 --> 00:00:57,812
Trusted contacts bypass AI processing.

11
00:00:57,812 --> 00:01:04,510
Unknown callers first meet a Front Door agent that asks who is calling and why.

12
00:01:04,510 --> 00:01:10,314
Once connected, separate audio legs give caller and senior transcripts a known identity.

13
00:01:10,314 --> 00:01:15,226
A deterministic risk engine combines phrases, time decay, and contextual bonuses.

14
00:01:15,226 --> 00:01:21,031
When intervention rules fire, the caller is held while Guardian offers private guidance.

15
00:01:21,031 --> 00:01:27,282
The dashboard exposes evidence and the timeline so the family can understand what happened.

16
00:01:28,282 --> 00:01:31,681
Assembly AI is central to the implementation.

17
00:01:31,681 --> 00:01:36,537
The Voice Agent API supports Front Door and Guardian conversations.

18
00:01:36,537 --> 00:01:42,849
Universal Streaming transcribes each connected audio leg, with keyterm prompting for relevant vocabulary.

19
00:01:42,849 --> 00:01:52,074
After an unknown call, batch processing requests redacted transcripts and audio, and LLM Gateway supports a structured incident summary.

20
00:01:52,074 --> 00:01:57,900
Groq, Gemini, and deterministic fallback paths help the prototype handle provider failures.

21
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:06,154
These fallbacks are disclosed because a working Replay alone does not prove that every hosted integration ran.

22
00:02:07,154 --> 00:02:13,976
Here is an actual recording of the local dashboard running Replay at twice normal speed.

23
00:02:13,976 --> 00:02:16,250
The entire story is scripted.

24
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:21,708
The stranger claims authority, threatens lost benefits, and asks for account numbers.

25
00:02:21,708 --> 00:02:24,436
Risk evidence accumulates alongside the conversation.

26
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:32,623
The timeline shows the intervention: the caller is paused, Guardian explains the concern, and Margaret asks for Sarah.

27
00:02:32,623 --> 00:02:38,535
The scripted story then brings Sarah into the conversation and ends the call.

28
00:02:38,535 --> 00:02:41,718
The dashboard preserves the sequence for review.

29
00:02:41,718 --> 00:02:50,814
This demonstrates the interface and event flow; it is not a real telephone call or a live speech recognition benchmark.

30
00:02:51,814 --> 00:02:57,702
The first customer hypothesis is an adult child supporting an older parent.

31
00:02:57,702 --> 00:03:04,081
A family subscription could offer reassurance and a shared view of concerning calls.

32
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:08,497
Potential partners include senior living organizations and financial services.

33
00:03:08,497 --> 00:03:12,914
These are hypotheses to validate through interviews and pilots.

34
00:03:12,914 --> 00:03:17,820
Pricing requires measured usage, carrier charges, infrastructure, and support costs.

35
00:03:17,820 --> 00:03:22,727
We have not demonstrated prevented financial losses or commercial demand.

36
00:03:23,727 --> 00:03:27,667
Our local test suite passes eighty five tests.

37
00:03:27,667 --> 00:03:34,562
An offline rules evaluation also covers sixty constructed scam scripts and forty benign scripts.

38
00:03:34,562 --> 00:03:39,980
All scam fixtures reached intervention, while the benign fixtures did not.

39
00:03:39,980 --> 00:03:47,368
These results check expected behavior on our examples; they do not establish real world accuracy.

40
00:03:47,368 --> 00:03:54,263
Public smoke checks passed for health, pages, dashboard connectivity, and the Replay event story.

41
00:03:54,263 --> 00:03:59,680
The next evaluation must include representative audio, accents, interruptions, and outages.

42
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:06,755
Today Eufisky is an English first browser simulation, with optional Spanish monitoring.

43
00:04:06,755 --> 00:04:12,324
Real phone integration through Twilio or another carrier is future work.

44
00:04:12,324 --> 00:04:21,436
Before real world use, priorities include consent hardening, authenticated households, stronger caller identity, and complete privacy lifecycle controls.

45
00:04:21,436 --> 00:04:26,499
We also need to measure false interventions and recovery behavior.

46
00:04:26,499 --> 00:04:32,574
Those steps turn a promising demonstration into evidence for a responsible pilot.

47
00:04:33,574 --> 00:04:40,547
Eufisky's focus is private assistance, understandable evidence, and a trusted person when a conversation becomes concerning.

48
00:04:40,547 --> 00:04:47,520
The public browser demo and source repository are linked in this deck and the submission copy.

49
00:04:47,520 --> 00:04:51,442
Try Replay to inspect the story without microphone setup.

50
00:04:51,442 --> 00:04:55,364
Thank you for reviewing Eufisky, built with Assembly AI.
